  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nLate evening radar and satellite imagery depicted strong to\nsevere outflow dominant/elevated thunderstorms, in response to a\nweak shortwave ejecting northeast from the Rockies, on the\nnorthwest periphery of the midlevel anticyclone centered near \nthe Ozarks. Storms have been prolific lightning producers, with\nwind gusts of 60-70 mph observed from the associated gust \nfronts. Nocturnal convective inhibition will eventually force \nstorms to weaken through the early morning hours. Pops were \nincreased from the NBM, favoring the northern zones, but recent\nradar shows storms weakening rapidly in the increasing\ninhibition. \n\nModels show little if any thermodynamic change Monday versus\nSunday, so persistence is a good forecast tool, using Sunday's\nhigh temperatures as a guide. Forecast max temperatures around\n4 pm Monday are within a degree of those observed Sunday, in the\n100-105 range, and the forecast lies several degrees above the \ncool-biased NBM. Given these preferred hotter temperatures,\napparent temperatures will be near heat advisory criteria\n(105-107) for the peak heating hours across the southeast\ncounties. MAV MOS guidance has been performing well in this\nheatwave, and the forecast follows this guidance closely. Winds\nwill be rather light Monday, which will serve to increase heat\nimpacts. \n\nTuesday and Wednesday, the heat dome midlevel high will be\ncentered well east of SW KS, with SW KS residing under the\nhigh's western periphery. Models show little thermal change \nfrom day to day, as such persistence will continue to be a good\ntemperature forecasting strategy. Afternoon temperatures of\n100-105 will remain common, and the repeatedly hot afternoons\nwill have a cumulative effect on heat-related stress. \n\nAll models show the heatwave intensifying, or at least\npersisting, Thursday and Friday, as the upper high retrogrades\nwestward, centered near Tulsa Thursday, and back over Kansas\nFriday. NBM is already forecasting widespread afternoon highs of\n100-102, and again this is almost certainly a few degrees too\nlow. A positive heat feedback will gradually take shape this \nweek, as repeated sunny hot dry afternoons dry out vegetation \nand bake the soil, which in turn encourages more hot afternoons.\n\nSaturday through Labor Day will feature an unseasonably hot,\nperhaps historically hot, Labor Day weekend. All global models\nand their ensembles allow the high to remain stationary over or\nnear SW KS, while intensifying to 598-600 dm. Temperatures will\neasily surpass 100 on a daily basis, about 15 degrees above\nnormal for early September. The entire NBM forecast through\nLabor Day weekend is correctly completely dry, in the very\nsubsident environment.",
